1
00:00:01,144 --> 00:00:09,224
When Marvel came calling for Daredevil: Born Again, almost every
cast member from the original Netflix era said yes without hesitation.

2
00:00:09,844 --> 00:00:11,514
Vincent D'Onofrio said yes.

3
00:00:11,924 --> 00:00:13,604
Wilson Bethel said yes.

4
00:00:14,143 --> 00:00:15,793
Jon Bernthal said yes.

5
00:00:16,473 --> 00:00:20,014
Krysten Ritter waited six years and then said yes.

6
00:00:20,534 --> 00:00:22,154
Deborah Ann Woll said no.

7
00:00:22,544 --> 00:00:24,874
Not because she did not want to come back.

8
00:00:25,134 --> 00:00:26,774
Not because she had moved on.

9
00:00:27,193 --> 00:00:34,373
She said no because she had spent years protecting a character
that Hollywood kept trying to reduce to a supporting role.

10
00:00:35,123 --> 00:00:40,153
And she was not going to hand that character back to a version
of the show that would waste her.

11
00:00:40,814 --> 00:00:44,904
What happened after that no, and what it took to turn it into a yes,

12
00:00:45,544 --> 00:00:50,774
is the most quietly powerful story in the entire Daredevil universe.

13
00:00:51,593 --> 00:00:53,463
And almost nobody is talking about it.

14
00:00:53,868 --> 00:00:55,698
She grew up in Brooklyn, New York.

15
00:00:56,078 --> 00:00:57,518
Her father was an architect.

16
00:00:57,948 --> 00:01:04,288
Her mother taught at the Berkeley Carroll School, a private school
in the same borough where Deborah would eventually find her footing.

17
00:01:04,768 --> 00:01:08,858
She started taking acting lessons in middle school
alongside piano and dance.

18
00:01:09,338 --> 00:01:11,548
The piano and the dance did not stick.

19
00:01:12,018 --> 00:01:13,158
The acting did.

20
00:01:13,908 --> 00:01:22,408
By the time she enrolled in the prestigious BFA program
at the University of Southern California,
she already knew this was the only path that made sense.

21
00:01:22,978 --> 00:01:25,298
After USC, she kept going.

22
00:01:25,718 --> 00:01:30,878
She flew to London for an intensive Shakespeare program
at the Royal Academy of Dramatic Arts.

23
00:01:31,248 --> 00:01:36,718
She came back and spent the next several years
doing exactly what drama schools tell you to do.

24
00:01:37,218 --> 00:01:42,718
Stage work, guest spots, small parts,
building the craft in the dark.

25
00:01:43,518 --> 00:01:44,288
Ten years.

26
00:01:44,858 --> 00:01:47,968
That is how long she worked before
True Blood changed everything.

27
00:01:48,378 --> 00:01:55,218
A decade of theater and bit parts and near misses
that most people will never know about
because none of it was visible yet.

28
00:01:56,468 --> 00:01:58,748
She showed up on Life in 2007.

29
00:01:59,038 --> 00:02:00,988
She had a single episode on ER.

30
00:02:01,388 --> 00:02:02,838
She appeared in CSI.

31
00:02:03,168 --> 00:02:06,618
She blinked across the screen in
My Name Is Earl and The Mentalist.

32
00:02:07,158 --> 00:02:16,228
And then HBO came along with a vampire show set in Louisiana,
and the person they cast for one episode
decided she was not going anywhere.

33
00:02:16,671 --> 00:02:19,211
True Blood premiered in 2008.

34
00:02:19,691 --> 00:02:34,891
The show was HBO at its most ambitious and unhinged,
a fantasy drama about vampires living openly in American society,
anchored by Anna Paquin as Sookie Stackhouse,
a telepathic waitress in rural Louisiana.

35
00:02:35,621 --> 00:02:40,721
Deborah Ann Woll was cast as Jessica Hamby
for a single episode in season one.

36
00:02:41,361 --> 00:02:52,361
The character was Bill Compton's newly turned vampire progeny,
a teenage girl who had been snatched out of her sheltered
religious upbringing and thrown into a life she never chose.

37
00:02:52,781 --> 00:02:55,501
She was supposed to appear once and disappear.

38
00:02:56,111 --> 00:02:57,191
The writers kept her.

39
00:02:57,661 --> 00:03:01,091
They saw something in Woll's performance
that was too interesting to discard.

40
00:03:01,881 --> 00:03:08,541
Jessica moved from a one-episode appearance to a recurring character,
and then from recurring to series regular.

41
00:03:09,391 --> 00:03:11,391
She stayed for all seven seasons.

42
00:03:11,881 --> 00:03:16,151
The ensemble won a Satellite Award for
Best Cast in a Television Series.

43
00:03:16,711 --> 00:03:20,261
Woll received a Screen Actors Guild Award nomination
alongside her castmates.

44
00:03:21,751 --> 00:03:31,751
For six years, she was one of the most recognizable faces
on one of the biggest shows on cable television,
and yet the industry still had not given her a lead role.

45
00:03:32,511 --> 00:03:34,801
True Blood was Anna Paquin's show.

46
00:03:35,581 --> 00:03:36,711
Woll was beloved in it.

47
00:03:37,121 --> 00:03:38,431
She was not the center.

48
00:03:39,091 --> 00:03:44,181
That pattern was about to end, but only because she drew a line
before she even walked through the door.

49
00:03:44,534 --> 00:03:51,954
In 2014, Marvel and Netflix came to her with an offer:
Karen Page in the new Daredevil series.

50
00:03:52,694 --> 00:03:55,814
Karen Page in the comics has a complicated history.

51
00:03:56,214 --> 00:03:57,544
She starts as a secretary.

52
00:03:57,994 --> 00:03:59,354
She becomes a love interest.

53
00:03:59,744 --> 00:04:08,604
In a famous 1999 storyline, she is killed off and stays dead,
with most writers since then having no particular interest
in bringing her back.

54
00:04:09,434 --> 00:04:13,754
Woll had one question before she agreed to anything:
Who is this character really?

55
00:04:14,544 --> 00:04:21,184
Because if Karen Page was going to be someone's girlfriend
and nothing else, she did not want the part.

56
00:04:21,804 --> 00:04:23,104
That is not a paraphrase.

57
00:04:23,544 --> 00:04:26,014
That is her direct quote from a panel years later.

58
00:04:26,314 --> 00:04:29,774
"If she's just going to be someone's girlfriend,
I don't want to do it."

59
00:04:30,604 --> 00:04:32,434
Marvel said yes to her terms.

60
00:04:32,964 --> 00:04:43,594
Karen Page in the Netflix series became a journalist, an investigator,
a woman with a buried past involving her brother's death
and a secret she had carried for years.

61
00:04:44,354 --> 00:04:52,464
She had her own moral code, her own story arcs,
her own reasons for being in Hell's Kitchen
that had nothing to do with Matt Murdock's feelings.

62
00:04:53,334 --> 00:04:58,864
Woll played her across three seasons of Daredevil,
through The Punisher, through The Defenders.

63
00:04:59,674 --> 00:05:10,404
Critics consistently named Karen as one of the strongest elements
of the Netflix Marvel universe, and Woll consistently spoke
about her the same way:

64
00:05:10,404 --> 00:05:18,394
not as a supporting character filling space around the lead,
but as a full person whose story existed
whether or not the cameras were pointing at her.

65
00:05:18,864 --> 00:05:22,454
Her quote on this became something fans repeated for years.

66
00:05:23,034 --> 00:05:29,324
"You might not see the Karen Page story, but she's having
a whole TV show on her own that no one filmed.

67
00:05:30,114 --> 00:05:33,474
Make sure that we just continue to honor
that they have full lives."

68
00:05:34,874 --> 00:05:43,304
Then Netflix canceled everything in 2019, and what she did during the years
that followed tells you almost everything about who she actually is.

69
00:05:43,970 --> 00:05:46,950
The silence after the Netflix cancellations was loud.

70
00:05:47,420 --> 00:05:51,410
Cox, D'Onofrio, Bernthal, Ritter, all waiting.

71
00:05:52,060 --> 00:05:56,760
Woll was waiting too, but she did not perform the waiting
the way some of the others did.

72
00:05:57,230 --> 00:06:00,770
There were no campaigns, no press tours saying she was ready.

73
00:06:01,520 --> 00:06:03,230
She went and made other things.

74
00:06:03,690 --> 00:06:06,430
She had been a Dungeons & Dragons player for years.

75
00:06:06,940 --> 00:06:10,160
During the quiet period, she went fully into that world.

76
00:06:10,590 --> 00:06:22,340
She appeared on Critical Role, the massively popular actual play web series
run by Matthew Mercer, as a guest character named Twiggy,
a gnome rogue with an arcane trickster subclass.

77
00:06:22,920 --> 00:06:25,530
She played in Force Grey: Lost City of Omu.

78
00:06:26,070 --> 00:06:28,790
She started dungeon mastering charity one-shots.

79
00:06:29,250 --> 00:06:35,150
She built a genuine creative community around tabletop gaming
that existed entirely outside of Hollywood.

80
00:06:36,540 --> 00:06:45,070
For a certain generation of viewers, Deborah Ann Woll is as well known
for what she does at a gaming table
as for anything she has done on screen.

81
00:06:45,720 --> 00:06:47,610
That is not an accident.

82
00:06:47,870 --> 00:06:53,310
It is what happens when someone who knows who they are
finds a space where they can be fully themselves.

83
00:06:54,690 --> 00:06:56,720
Away from the table, she kept acting.

84
00:06:57,320 --> 00:07:04,210
Escape Room in 2019 and its sequel,
Tournament of Champions, in 2021.

85
00:07:05,070 --> 00:07:22,560
In 2022, she did the voice and full-motion capture work for Faye in God of War,
a major AAA video game role that most people outside the gaming world did not notice,
but which required the same depth of emotional performance as any screen role.

86
00:07:23,680 --> 00:07:26,340
Queen of the Ring in 2024.

87
00:07:27,310 --> 00:07:37,040
She also never stopped advocating for her husband, E.J. Scott,
who has choroideremia, a rare inherited condition
that causes progressive blindness.

88
00:07:37,690 --> 00:07:38,640
There is no cure.

89
00:07:39,250 --> 00:07:43,370
Woll has used her platform consistently
to raise money and awareness.

90
00:07:43,900 --> 00:07:56,920
She has talked publicly about her own celiac disease, about navigating chronic illness
while maintaining a career, about the ways that personal life
and professional life do not sit in separate boxes.

91
00:07:58,320 --> 00:08:02,490
None of this is the behavior of someone who was simply waiting
for Hollywood to remember her.

92
00:08:03,090 --> 00:08:10,740
It is the behavior of someone who had decided quietly
that her life was not on hold,
and then the call came, and she said no.

93
00:08:12,241 --> 00:08:20,351
When Marvel Studios began developing Daredevil: Born Again,
the original version of the show did not include Karen Page.

94
00:08:20,771 --> 00:08:23,121
It did not include Foggy Nelson either.

95
00:08:23,791 --> 00:08:41,111
Reports from inside the production suggested the plan was to build a leaner,
more MCU-integrated action series, one that used the recognizable faces
of Cox and D'Onofrio but moved away from the grounded legal drama world
that had defined the Netflix run.

96
00:08:41,681 --> 00:08:44,451
Fans reacted. Critics reacted.

97
00:08:44,951 --> 00:08:50,741
And in September 2023, news broke that the show
was being overhauled mid-production.

98
00:08:51,181 --> 00:08:53,801
Scripts scrapped. Writers replaced.

99
00:08:54,341 --> 00:09:02,681
A new showrunner brought in, Dario Scardapane, with a specific mandate
to reintegrate the soul of the original series.

100
00:09:03,881 --> 00:09:06,051
Scardapane wanted Karen back.

101
00:09:06,231 --> 00:09:07,321
He approached Woll.

102
00:09:07,771 --> 00:09:08,551
She said no.

103
00:09:08,841 --> 00:09:17,951
She has been careful in how she has discussed this publicly,
but the shape of what happened is clear from what she has said
at panels and in interviews.

104
00:09:18,641 --> 00:09:21,631
She had a lot of loyalty to this character and this story.

105
00:09:22,181 --> 00:09:31,291
The original Born Again had not included Karen at all, meaning the production
had already signaled once that the character was dispensable.

106
00:09:32,071 --> 00:09:38,291
Woll was not willing to return just to fill a small role
in a story where Karen was an afterthought.

107
00:09:39,141 --> 00:09:41,531
She had set her terms in 2014.

108
00:09:42,071 --> 00:09:43,631
They had not changed.

109
00:09:44,101 --> 00:09:45,901
Scardapane came back with a pitch.

110
00:09:46,161 --> 00:09:53,541
He had ideas for Karen that were bigger, more central,
more in keeping with the character Woll had always insisted she was.

111
00:09:54,041 --> 00:09:55,471
He sold her on the story.

112
00:09:55,881 --> 00:10:04,281
She said yes, but not before making sure the version of Karen
she was agreeing to was the one that deserved to exist.

113
00:10:05,501 --> 00:10:10,511
Born Again season one aired in March 2025.

114
00:10:11,071 --> 00:10:16,521
Karen appeared in three episodes: episode one
and then again toward the end of the season.

115
00:10:17,081 --> 00:10:20,241
Her absence from the middle of the show was written into the story.

116
00:10:20,811 --> 00:10:21,861
Foggy had died.

117
00:10:22,171 --> 00:10:24,321
Matt had withdrawn into his grief.

118
00:10:24,611 --> 00:10:29,771
And Karen had done what she always does
when everything collapses around her. She ran.

119
00:10:30,411 --> 00:10:34,071
It was, Woll said later, completely true to the character.

120
00:10:34,711 --> 00:10:40,801
When Karen is at her strongest, she dives in headfirst.
When she is at her lowest, she runs.

121
00:10:41,331 --> 00:10:43,871
The absence had logic. It had weight.

122
00:10:44,301 --> 00:10:47,391
And it set up something much bigger in the season that followed.

123
00:10:48,857 --> 00:10:51,387
Daredevil: Born Again.

124
00:10:52,267 --> 00:10:59,177
Season two aired in 2026, and this time Karen Page
was not a supporting character.

125
00:10:59,607 --> 00:11:01,127
She was not someone's girlfriend.

126
00:11:01,547 --> 00:11:05,897
She was not a figure who existed to reflect
the lead's emotional state back at him.

127
00:11:06,907 --> 00:11:12,417
Scardapane described what he built for Karen in season two
as co-parenting Hell's Kitchen.

128
00:11:13,197 --> 00:11:24,707
Karen and Matt working in near perfect sync, sharing intelligence,
reaching the same conclusion simultaneously, and then splitting off
to handle their respective sides of the same problem.

129
00:11:25,187 --> 00:11:28,127
She kidnaps a federal agent in episode three.

130
00:11:28,687 --> 00:11:31,387
She makes decisions in the field without asking permission.

131
00:11:31,977 --> 00:11:36,457
The showrunner's words in interviews were the same ones
Woll had been saying for years.

132
00:11:36,987 --> 00:11:43,617
"In my mind and in Debs's mind, she has never been a sidekick.
She has never been a girlfriend."

133
00:11:44,847 --> 00:11:53,537
That line said by the showrunner, not by Woll herself, is the clearest possible signal
of what her decade-long insistence had produced.

134
00:11:54,207 --> 00:11:57,577
She had said it to Marvel in 2014 before she took the role.

135
00:11:58,107 --> 00:12:03,317
She had said it when Netflix canceled the show,
and she spoke about Karen's dignity in interviews.

136
00:12:04,017 --> 00:12:12,087
She had said it by turning down the first Born Again offer,
and eventually the show got a showrunner
who had absorbed that message completely.

137
00:12:12,817 --> 00:12:17,377
In the comics, Karen Page has been dead since 1999.

138
00:12:17,867 --> 00:12:19,487
Nobody has brought her back.

139
00:12:20,157 --> 00:12:30,657
The version of Karen Page that exists on screen, the journalist, the investigator,
the woman co-running a secret operation against the kingpin of New York

140
00:12:31,277 --> 00:12:45,527
exists because Deborah Ann Woll fought for her at every stage:
before she took the role, during the run, after the cancellation,
when Marvel came back with the wrong version
and when they finally came back with the right one.

141
00:12:46,807 --> 00:12:55,497
There is a version of this story where Woll says "Yes" the first time Marvel calls,
shows up for a few episodes of Born Again,

142
00:12:55,497 --> 00:13:00,457
and the character fades quietly into the background
of a franchise that was never really built around her.

143
00:13:01,007 --> 00:13:04,497
That version did not happen because she refused to let it.

144
00:13:04,937 --> 00:13:09,257
Some actors fight for attention, some fight for billing,
some fight for money.

145
00:13:10,507 --> 00:13:19,397
Deborah Ann Woll spent 10 years fighting for a fictional woman's right
to have a full life, and in the end,
the industry came around to her way of thinking.

146
00:13:19,897 --> 00:13:21,447
It just needed a little convincing.
